## 4. Interface identification and coupling gains

The directed coupling graph has the following edges:

| Edge | Source | Target | Flux | Gain bound |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| $S \to E$ | Module S | Module E | $g(X, A)$ (assimilation support) | $\|G_{ES}\| = L_E$ |
| $E \to S$ | Module E | Module S | $d_U(U)$ (decomposition return) | $\|G_{SE}\| = L_A^{(U)}$ |
| $E \to S$ | Module E | Module S | $-g(X, A)$ (assimilation drawdown) | $\|G_{SE}\| = L_A^{(X)}$ |
| $E \to P$ | Module E | Module P | $(1-\alpha) h(X, E)$ (harvest) | $\|G_{PE}\| = (1-\alpha) h_{\max}$ |
| $S \to P$ | Module S | Module P | $c_G(G, E_G)$ (mining) | $\|G_{PS}\| = c_{G,\max}$ |
| $P \to E$ | Module P | Module E | $\rho r_P(P)$ (recycling) | $\|G_{EP}\| = \rho r_{P,\max}$ |
| $P \to W$ | Module P | Sink $W$ | $(1-\rho) r_P(P)$ (retirement loss) | $\|G_{WP}\| = (1-\rho) r_{P,\max}$ |

